# Agent tab indicator (see docs/agent-tab-indicator.md). Claude Code / Codex
# hooks + scripts/agent-tab-watcher.sh maintain per-window options
# @agent_state (idle|running|needs-input|failed|done), @agent_summary,
# @agent_workflow (a background Claude Workflow OR a background subagent in
# flight - one gear for both) and @agent_cua (the agent is driving an app
# through cua-driver); the Catppuccin window formats below render them.
#
# THREE CHANNELS. Each tab says three independent things, one per surface:
#   NUMBER CHIP ($color)      motion — is this window in flight right now?
#   TAB BODY    ($background) attention tier — does it want you? (green/
#                             yellow/red fill, ambient states leave it stock)
#   GLYPH SLOT  (text)        the exception — a job the chip alone can't
#                             describe: 󰒓 background workflow or subagent,
#                             󰍽 computer use.
#                             EMPTY for idle and for an ordinary running turn.
# There is deliberately no "an agent lives here" marker anymore (the old mauve
# robot): an idle agent tab is a stock blue tab. Presence is still legible from
# the tab NAME, which reads project/short-title for agent windows and #W
# (zsh, nvim, …) for everything else.
#
# SHARED PALETTE — these hues are the tab-bar half of one agent-state color
# language; CuaNotch's glow renders the SAME state in the same hue, saturated
# for light instead of pastel-on-dark (see the memory note / CuaNotch.swift):
#   idle/ready      grey   #6c7086  ·  notch: no glow at all
#   working         pink   #f5c2e7  ·  notch magenta #FF4FD1  (subtle)
#   computer use    blue   #89b4fa  ·  notch blue   #408CFF   (full)
#   needs approval  red    #f38ba8  ·  notch red    #FF4740   (full)
#   waiting input   yellow #f9e2af  ·  notch amber  #FF9E26   (full)
#   done, unvisited green  #a6e3a1  ·  notch green  #33E673   (full→subtle)
#   workflow        teal   #94e2d5  ·  notch: (none)
# Emphasis tiers match too: the states that WANT YOU (approval/input/done)
# fill the whole tab, exactly as they burn at full strength in the glow;
# ambient states (working/cua/idle) never touch the body, as they glow subtly.
# Motion means the same thing in both: pulsing = in flight, steady = waiting
# on you. Don't reassign a hue in one surface without the other.
#
# ONE EXCEPTION to "same hue everywhere": blue #89b4fa is BOTH catppuccin's
# resting chip accent and the computer-use hue. On the chip, blue therefore
# reads as "at rest", so the working pulse pink↔blue reads as working↔resting
# rather than as a third state — and computer use is carried by the 󰍽 glyph in
# the state's blue instead. That is why cua lives in the glyph slot and not in
# the chip.
#
# Background tint: Catppuccin pastes @catppuccin_window_*_background verbatim
# into ALL FOUR tab segments that use $background (number fg, middle-sep bg,
# text bg, right-sep fg), so injecting a per-window conditional here tints the
# whole tab consistently — tinting only the text segment would leave stale
# separator end-caps. The expression must stay space-free and quote-free
# (catppuccin's option batch reader splits on spaces and strips quotes).
# Mocha: done=green #a6e3a1, failed=red #f38ba8, needs-input=yellow
# #f9e2af. running/cua get NO bg change (ambient tier — their signal is the
# pulsing number chip in the $color options below). A
# done tab with a background workflow still in flight is NOT tinted green
# (it isn't really finished) — it falls back to stock and the blinking gear
# glyph carries the signal. No-state fallbacks (catppuccin stock): SELECTED
# tab sits on base #1e1e2e (blends into the status bar) and unselected tabs
# get the surface0 #313244 gray pill.
#
# @stash_sel is the range-selection tint (prefix+v, see the
# bindings near the bottom) and it wins over every agent state: while you are
# choosing what to park, "about to be hidden" is the only thing the tab needs to
# say, and it is a transient mode rather than a status. Mauve #cba6f7 is the one
# accent nothing else claims as a BODY colour — pink #f5c2e7 is close but only
# ever appears as a chip — so a mauve tab is unambiguous.
set -g @catppuccin_window_default_background "#{?#{@stash_sel},#cba6f7,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#{?#{@agent_workflow},#313244,#a6e3a1},#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},failed},#f38ba8,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},needs-input},#f9e2af,#313244}}}}"
set -g @catppuccin_window_current_background "#{?#{@stash_sel},#cba6f7,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#{?#{@agent_workflow},#1e1e2e,#a6e3a1},#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},failed},#f38ba8,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},needs-input},#f9e2af,#1e1e2e}}}}"

# fill=number locks the window NUMBER's fg to the $background expression
# above (number block is `#[fg=$background,bg=$color]#I`), which would put a
# pastel digit on the blue/orange accent — illegible (WCAG CR 1.2-1.7). Darken
# the accent ($color: also the left cap and middle-sep stroke) to crust
# #11111b on attention states so the state-colored digit reads against it.
#
# $color is ALSO the motion channel: an UNSELECTED tab whose window is in
# flight (running || cua || workflow) pulses its number chip pink #f5c2e7 ↔
# blue #89b4fa at 1 s, driven by the same global @agent_blink the watcher
# already toggles. Blue is the resting accent, so the pulse reads as
# working↔resting.
#
# PINK, not mauve (2026-08-19 — mauve read as too subtle, and the numbers say
# why): mauve #cba6f7 and blue #89b4fa have near-identical relative luminance
# (0.467 vs 0.449) and sit in the same blue-violet family, so the pulse moved
# hue only and barely registered. Pink is L 0.638 — a 1.42:1 BRIGHTNESS step
# on top of a ~100° hue swing, so the chip visibly lightens as well as shifts.
# It is also the only mocha hue left: lavender/sky/sapphire are the blue
# family (mauve's failure, worse), rosewater/flamingo/maroon are the red
# family. Pink sits 27° off failure red #f38ba8, which sounds close but
# cannot collide — attention states force the chip to CRUST, so a red chip and
# a pink chip never exist on the same surface (and pink is far lighter, 0.638
# vs 0.404). Digit contrast improves too: 8.2:1 on pink vs 6.2:1 on mauve.
# Priority: attention beats motion — needs-*/done force crust
# regardless, because the digit's own fg is the bright body tint and needs a
# dark chip to read at all. (A permission prompt raised while a background
# workflow runs is therefore a red tab with a still chip and a gear glyph.)
# done+workflow is not tinted, so it pulses like any other in-flight window.
# The SELECTED tab never pulses: it is solid peach for every ambient state —
# you are looking at it, the terminal itself is the progress indicator, and a
# blinking chip under your cursor is just noise.
#
# @stash_sel rides the SAME "use dark ink" test that needs-*/failed use, here
# and in the _text options below (8 places in all). That one test is what makes
# the range tint work without a second set of colour rules: the mauve body is a
# bright tint like theirs, so everything drawn ON it — chip, glyphs, label —
# needs crust for the same reason and by the same branch. Tinting the body
# alone would have left a done tab's dark text on… a light background it was
# already dark for, but an ordinary tab's #cdd6f4 label unreadable on mauve.
set -g @catppuccin_window_default_color "#{?#{@stash_sel},#11111b,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#{?#{@agent_workflow},#{?#{@agent_blink},#f5c2e7,#89b4fa},#11111b},#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#{?#{@agent_workflow},#{?#{@agent_blink},#f5c2e7,#89b4fa},#{?#{@agent_cua},#{?#{@agent_blink},#f5c2e7,#89b4fa},#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},running},#{?#{@agent_blink},#f5c2e7,#89b4fa},#89b4fa}}}}}}"
set -g @catppuccin_window_current_color "#{?#{@stash_sel},#11111b,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#{?#{@agent_workflow},#fab387,#11111b},#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#fab387}}}"

# Tab text: the glyph slot, then the summary. The slot is EXCEPTIONS ONLY —
# priority background workflow > computer use > nothing. Ordinary states
# (idle, running, and every attention state) render NO glyph: the chip pulse
# says "in flight" and the body tint says "wants you", so a robot or a ● would
# only restate what the colors already carry, and both cost 2 cells.
#   󰒓 (nf-md-cog U+F0493)   teal #94e2d5 — a background Workflow outliving the
#                           turn; the one thing no other channel encodes.
#   󰍽 (nf-md-mouse U+F037D) blue #89b4fa — the agent is driving an app through
#                           cua-driver (see the palette note above for why cua
#                           lives here and not in the chip).
# The slot is EXCLUSIVE and the gear wins: a session both driving an app and
# running a workflow shows only 󰒓. Deliberate, and deliberately asymmetric with
# CuaNotch, where a workflow is an OVERLAY that composes with the state and the
# popup row shows both (blue dot + teal gear). The gear is the one that must
# survive — it is the ONLY carrier of "a fleet is still out" and it outlives the
# turn, so dropping it lets a finished-looking tab hide live work; the mouse
# only refines a chip that is already pulsing "in flight". Both glyphs (4 cells)
# was considered and rejected: double the width jitter for a rare pair, and the
# notch popup is the detail surface for precisely this case.
# agent-tab-watcher.sh toggles the global @agent_blink each second while
# anything is running/driving/workflowing; each pulsing glyph alternates
# between its hue and a ~68% BRIGHTNESS copy of that same hue (dim blue
# #5d7aaa, dim teal #659a91). Never blink a GLYPH between two different hues:
# mid-pulse it then reads as some other state (this is why the chip, whose
# resting blue means "no agent working", can pulse mauve↔blue and a glyph
# cannot). On a TINTED tab the glyph goes crust #11111b and stops pulsing —
# teal on yellow/red is unreadable, and a tab already at maximum urgency does
# not need a second animation. Then a fg readable on the bright attention
# backgrounds (crust #11111b; light #cdd6f4 when a workflow suppressed the
# done tint), then the agent summary ("project/short title") with #W fallback.
# Tabs are NOT width-stabilised: the slot's 2 cells appear and vanish with the
# workflow/cua flags and shove later tabs. Deliberate — those flags flip rarely
# (once per workflow, not per turn), and reserving a blank for every agent
# window would pad the common case to pay for the rare one.
set -g @catppuccin_window_default_text "#{?#{@agent_workflow},#[fg=#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#{?#{@agent_blink},#94e2d5,#659a91}}]󰒓 ,#{?#{@agent_cua},#[fg=#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#11111b,#{?#{@agent_blink},#89b4fa,#5d7aaa}}}]󰍽 ,}}#[fg=#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#{?#{@agent_workflow},#cdd6f4,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#11111b,#cdd6f4}}}]#{?#{n:#{@agent_summary}},#{@agent_summary},#W}"
set -g @catppuccin_window_current_text "#{?#{@agent_workflow},#[fg=#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#{?#{@agent_blink},#94e2d5,#659a91}}]󰒓 ,#{?#{@agent_cua},#[fg=#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#11111b,#{?#{@agent_blink},#89b4fa,#5d7aaa}}}]󰍽 ,}}#[fg=#{?#{||:#{@stash_sel},#{||:#{m:needs-*,#{@agent_state}},#{==:#{@agent_state},failed}}},#11111b,#{?#{@agent_workflow},#cdd6f4,#{?#{==:#{@agent_state},done},#11111b,#cdd6f4}}}]#{?#{n:#{@agent_summary}},#{@agent_summary},#W}"
# end catppuccin

# Agent tab indicator runtime: presence/GC watcher daemon (singleton), and
# focus discharges a tab's attention state (needs-input/done → idle) the
# moment you switch to it - and a yellow/green that lands while you are
# already sitting on that tab with WezTerm frontmost never tints at all (the
# hook and the watcher both test that). NOTE: this occupies after-select-window[0]; new
# handlers must use explicit indices ([1]+) or they'll overwrite this one.
run-shell -b "bash #{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/agent-tab-watcher.sh"
set-hook -g after-select-window 'run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/agent-tab-indicator.sh clear-current #{window_id}"'
# CuaNotch (notch glow indicator): instant visit acknowledgment — dismisses a
# finished agent's green / subsides a needs-input blaze the moment its tab is
# focused. Explicit [1] index to coexist with agent-tab-indicator above.
set-hook -g 'after-select-window[1]' 'run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.local/bin/cua-notch-visit"'
set-hook -g 'after-select-pane[1]' 'run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.local/bin/cua-notch-visit"'
set-hook -g 'client-session-changed[1]' 'run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.local/bin/cua-notch-visit"'

# Fork of tmux-plugins/tmux-resurrect. Its ONLY functional change is
# strategies/nvim_session.sh: upstream looks for a `Session.vim` in the pane's
# cwd (the tpope/vim-obsession convention), but we use folke/persistence.nvim,
# which writes to ~/.local/state/nvim/sessions/ with `/` encoded as `%`. Upstream
# therefore never matched a session file and restored a bare `nvim` with no
# buffers; the fork reads persistence.nvim's layout and emits `nvim -S <file>`.
# Upstream has been frozen since 2023-03; the fork is 0 behind / 4 ahead of it.
# Safe swap: resurrect self-registers @resurrect-{save,restore}-script-path from
# its own $CURRENT_DIR, so continuum and tmux-assistant-resurrect (which couple
# only through tmux options, not the plugin dir name) follow automatically.
set -g @plugin 'trevin-j/tmux-resurrect-nvim'
set -g @resurrect-dir '{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.tmux/resurrect'
set -g @resurrect-capture-pane-contents 'on'
set -g @resurrect-strategy-nvim 'session'
# Restore nu tabs as nu, not as the default shell. A `bind N` window runs nu as
# its command rather than a shell, so without this resurrect sees an unlisted
# process and respawns the pane with $SHELL (zsh) — the tab would come back
# silently demoted. @resurrect-processes APPENDS to the built-in default list
# (see _restore_list in scripts/process_restore_helpers.sh), so the defaults
# for nvim/less/htop/etc. are preserved.
set -g @resurrect-processes 'nu'
set -g @plugin 'tmux-plugins/tmux-continuum'
set -g @continuum-save-interval '15'
set -g @continuum-restore 'on'

# Persist and resume assistant CLI sessions with tmux-resurrect/continuum.
set -g @plugin 'timvw/tmux-assistant-resurrect'
set -g @assistant-resurrect-capture-env 'VIRTUAL_ENV NODE_ENV CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV OPENCODE_CONFIG'

unbind-key a
bind-key a display-popup -E -w 60% -h 75% "$HOME/.config/tmux/scripts/mru-session-switch.sh"

unbind-key C-a
bind-key C-a display-popup -E -w 60% -h 75% "$HOME/.config/tmux/scripts/mru-session-switch.sh"

set -g @plugin '27medkamal/tmux-session-wizard'
set -g @session-wizard 'T'

set -g @plugin 'sainnhe/tmux-fzf'
TMUX_FZF_LAUNCH_KEY="C-m"

set -g @plugin 'omerxx/tmux-floax'
set -g @floax-bind '-n M-q'

unbind -n M-a
bind -n M-a run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/toggle-btop-popup.sh '#{client_tty}'"

unbind -n M-y

# bind -n m-w send-keys -F "yy" Enter

# bind -n m-q run-shell "~/code/various_scripts/popuptmux"

# Other examples:
# set -g @plugin 'github_username/plugin_name'
# set -g @plugin 'github_username/plugin_name#branch'
# set -g @plugin 'git@github.com:user/plugin'
# set -g @plugin 'git@bitbucket.com:user/plugin'


# Park a tab out of the tab bar without stopping it (tmux has no hide/minimize
# of its own — tmux/tmux#3047 — but move-window to a detached session is exactly
# that). prefix+H hides, prefix+h brings one back; both verified unbound first.
# Not `run-shell -b` for unstash: it may need to raise a popup for the picker,
# and a backgrounded run-shell has no client to raise it on.
bind-key H run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh stash '#{window_id}'"
bind-key h run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh unstash"

# ...and a range of adjacent tabs at once, vim-visual style. prefix+v enters
# the mode with just the current tab selected (tinted); the client is then
# parked in the `stash` key table below, where h/l extend the selection and H
# — the same key that hides one tab — hides the lot. One key to enter, the
# same hide key to finish. The bring-back picker takes the nearest gesture
# fzf allows (Shift-Up/Down or Tab — a plain letter there is filter input).
#
# '#{window_id}' is passed for the same reason prefix+H passes it: a bare
# `display-message -p` inside the script does NOT resolve to the client's
# session, it resolves by tmux's most-recently-active rules — and once anything
# is parked, the holding session is the newest thing on the server, so every
# selection silently landed on it and did nothing.
#
# switch-client FIRST, run-shell second, and the order is load-bearing. A
# non-repeat binding resets the client to the root table at dispatch, and a
# foreground run-shell blocks the client's command queue until the script exits
# (~110-170ms: 7-10 tmux round-trips at ~15ms each). With the switch last, the
# client sits in ROOT for that whole window — where l is just a letter, so
# holding the key down types into the pane, and a quick H reaches it as a
# literal keystroke. Arming the table before the work closes the gap: a repeat
# then dispatches from the `stash` table and queues behind the running job,
# which is exactly the serialisation the foreground run-shell is here for.
bind-key v switch-client -T stash \; run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-start here '#{window_id}'"

# NOT run-shell -b: two quick taps backgrounded both read the same cursor and
# both wrote cursor+1, so the selection stopped growing while the keys kept
# registering. Foreground items go through the client's command queue in order,
# which serialises them; the freeze that makes that dangerous elsewhere needs a
# job that blocks for seconds, and these are a handful of tmux calls.
#
# Each extend re-arms the table: a key table is one-shot, so a binding that does
# not switch-client back into it drops you to root after one press.
# h/l, vim-style, once in the table.
bind-key -T stash l switch-client -T stash \; run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-move right '#{window_id}'"
bind-key -T stash h switch-client -T stash \; run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-move left '#{window_id}'"
bind-key -T stash H     run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-commit '#{window_id}'"
bind-key -T stash Enter run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-commit '#{window_id}'"
bind-key -T stash Escape run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-cancel"
bind-key -T stash q      run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-cancel"
bind-key -T stash C-c    run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-cancel"
bind-key -T stash Space  run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh sel-cancel"

# NO `Any` FALLBACK, deliberately — it was here and it made the mode unusable.
#
# `Any` matches literally every key event, and tmux's key events include things
# no human pressed. Its dispatch tries Any BEFORE the built-in bail-out that
# stops mouse movement from knocking a client out of a key table, so with
# `mouse on` a single cell of pointer movement cancelled the selection — and a
# Claude pane turns on all-motion tracking (DEC 1003), so in `main`, which is
# exactly where tabs get parked, the gesture only survived if the mouse never
# twitched. `focus-events on` is worse still: Any ate FocusOut, so Cmd-Tabbing
# away — or any agent on this machine moving a window — cancelled it too, and
# swallowed the focus event the pane's app was waiting for.
#
# Shadowing those with explicit no-op bindings is NOT available: tmux 3.6
# rejects `MouseMovePane`, `FocusIn` and `FocusOut` as unknown key names
# (checked). So the fallback goes, and an unbound key now falls through to root
# and reaches the pane, leaving the tint painted.
#
# That is survivable because the tint is self-healing rather than modal: every
# sel-start clears the flag server-wide before selecting, and a single
# prefix+H clears it too (see do_stash), and prefix+v restarts it. The one
# escape this cannot catch is the prefix key itself, which tmux intercepts
# before any binding lookup — so a binding for it in THIS table would never
# run. prefix followed by either of those is therefore the way out.

bind C-l kill-pane
# Initialize TMUX plugin manager (keep this line at the very bottom of tmux.conf)
run '~/.tmux/plugins/tpm/tpm'

# After tpm, because catppuccin builds status-right while tpm sources it — this
# prepends the stash indicator to the left end of it. See the script for why it
# is not a catppuccin module.
run-shell "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash-statusline.sh"

# --- resurrect save guard (must stay BELOW the tpm line) ---
set -g @resurrect-hook-post-save-all "bash '{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/tmux/plugins/tmux-assistant-resurrect/scripts/save-assistant-sessions.sh'; bash '{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/tmux/scripts/resurrect-guard.sh'"

# Parked windows come back parked on their own — resurrect saves the holding
# session verbatim — but it does not save WINDOW OPTIONS, which is where the
# parked state lives, including the sessionId of a suspended agent (the only
# surviving pointer to that conversation). This re-applies them afterwards.
#
# `set -ag` APPENDS: restore-assistant-sessions.sh is already on this hook and
# must keep running. And this sits below the tpm line for the same reason the
# guard above does — assistant-resurrect's .tmux re-sets these hooks when tpm
# loads it, so anything above would be silently replaced.
set -ag @resurrect-hook-post-restore-all "; bash '{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh' restore-state"

# The same options gap bites the `agents` session harder: tmux-pty-mcp owns
# its windows by @pty_* options, so a restore brings back every agent shell as
# an unownable idle zsh (80 of them on 2026-08-29). Prune those on arrival;
# the server's own sweep is the backstop if this hook ever doesn't run.
set -ag @resurrect-hook-post-restore-all "; bash '{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/tmux/scripts/agent-restore-prune.sh'"

# Keep the sidecar's key from going stale. It records windows by index, and
# `renumber-windows on` means closing ANY window shifts its neighbours — so a
# close the script did not perform left a row pointing at what is now a
# different window, and a later restore stamped a suspended session's id onto
# it. Refreshing on close means the index is never wrong for long.
#
# This fires once per window UNLINKED, so a range park fires N+1 of these,
# concurrently and unlocked, on top of whatever the park itself is doing. That
# used to be described here as safe because "save_state merges rather than
# rebuilds, so an interleaved snapshot cannot drop a session id" — which was
# wrong: the merge has a "still a live agent, so it needs no record" branch, and
# suspend_agent mirrors the id while the agent is still alive, so an interleaved
# snapshot dropped exactly the row that was about to become the only pointer to
# a conversation. save_state now takes its own lock and snapshots inside it,
# which is what actually makes this safe.
set-hook -g 'window-unlinked[1]' 'run-shell -b "#{HOME}/.config/tmux/scripts/stash.sh publish"'
